## Part E: Install and configure ngrok

### Step 9: Create ngrok folder

```
mkdir -p ~/ngrok-server
cd ~/ngrok-server
```

Check your Ubuntu architecture:

```
dpkg --print-architecture
```

If the output is `amd64`, run:

```
wget https://bin.equinox.io/c/bNyj1mQVY4c/ngrok-v3-stable-linux-amd64.tgz -O ngrok.tgz
```

If the output is `arm64`, run:

```
wget https://bin.equinox.io/c/bNyj1mQVY4c/ngrok-v3-stable-linux-arm64.tgz -O ngrok.tgz
```

Extract ngrok:

```
tar -xzf ngrok.tgz
chmod +x ngrok
```

Check installation:

```
./ngrok version
```

### Step 10: Authenticate ngrok

1. Open [ngrok signup](https://dashboard.ngrok.com/signup?utm_source=chatgpt.com).
2. Create or log in to your account.
3. Open the [Your Authtoken page](https://dashboard.ngrok.com/get-started/your-authtoken?utm_source=chatgpt.com).
4. Copy your authentication token.

In Ubuntu, run:

```
./ngrok config add-authtoken YOUR_NGROK_AUTH_TOKEN
```

Replace the placeholder with your actual token. Keep it private.

### Step 11: Start the backend tunnel

Make sure Apache is running:

```
sudo systemctl start apache2
```

Run:

```
cd ~/ngrok-server
./ngrok http 80
```

You will get a public forwarding URL similar to:

```
https://abcd-1234.ngrok-free.app
```

Backend publicly accessible

Keep this Ubuntu Terminal open. The tunnel must remain running.

Your backend URL is:

```
https://YOUR-NGROK-URL/contact-api/submit.php
```

Replace `YOUR-NGROK-URL` with the actual ngrok address.

